WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:01.890
- Well, let's call in my first guest,

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:05.000
he's the front man of Queen, no small feat

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.910
and a former finalist on American Idol.

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:10.280
You can see him streaming
across the globe on January 29th

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:12.590
and Adam Lambert live at the Roxy.

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:14.892
Give it up for Adam Lambert everybody.

00:00:14.892 --> 00:00:17.725
(audience cheers)

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:21.644
What's up you handsome devil?
Look how good you look.

00:00:21.644 --> 00:00:22.660
- What's up (laughs) ?

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:24.960
- I mean, I have to ask you this,

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:28.520
are you ever gonna come
out with a makeup line?

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:30.610
'Cause you do your makeup so well.

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:33.653
- Well, thanks, I'd love to,
I think that'd be really fun.

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:36.593
I've been playing with makeup
since I was a little kid.

00:00:36.593 --> 00:00:38.900
I remember 'cause I was in theater,

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:41.280
so it was part of
getting ready for a show.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.850
And I just loved getting to
sit in front of the mirror

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:45.120
and transform my face,

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:48.140
and cover up all my freckles at the time

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:50.430
'cause I was ashamed of them.

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:52.130
I wish I had them today but...

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:54.363
I love makeup, I think it's so much fun.

00:00:54.363 --> 00:00:56.640
- I wanted freckles so badly

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:57.950
because they said when I was a kid

00:00:57.950 --> 00:01:00.300
I used to hear people say
freckles were angel kisses,

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:04.000
and I was like, "I've never
been kissed by an angel,

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.153
I don't have one freckle."

00:01:05.153 --> 00:01:05.986
(both laugh)

00:01:05.986 --> 00:01:07.520
It's like, I used to be so down about it.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:10.350
But every time I run into you,
you always look so handsome,

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:13.860
and I'm always like "Is that
what I'm supposed to be doing?

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:17.206
(laughs) I'm I supposed
to be able to do that?"

00:01:17.206 --> 00:01:19.140
(laughs) Anyway, you look amazing but...

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:22.560
Did you see Lou Berlin's
song? Did you see that?

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.230
- Yes, yes, I was like,
"Now I need to see more,"

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:27.053
because I didn't understand
what it was at first,

00:01:27.053 --> 00:01:28.930
I just watched the song and
I'm like, "What's he saying?"

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:31.317
And then I saw on the top of the screen,

00:01:31.317 --> 00:01:34.737
the screenshot of the feud and I was like,

00:01:34.737 --> 00:01:36.777
"Oh my God, that's brilliant."

00:01:36.777 --> 00:01:40.400
- Have you ever been in
a virtual feud like that?

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.745
- I mean, probably.

00:01:42.745 --> 00:01:45.162
(both laugh)

00:01:46.444 --> 00:01:51.444
I learned a few years back
that what could be considered

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:55.210
a healthy debate for
some, is not for others.

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:57.720
And, when you're doing it on Twitter,

00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:59.360
you're really leaving yourself open

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:02.250
to all those different versions

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:05.167
of how they feel about a debate so.

00:02:05.167 --> 00:02:07.940
I know I try to keep it for
a private audience (laughs) .

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:12.120
- It is a hard thing because
I think sarcasm gets lost.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:13.500
A lot of things can get lost

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.450
when you're being funny or whatever,

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:18.970
and everybody always has
their different world

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:20.070
and how they're receiving it,

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:22.010
and, so it's so hard.

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:25.690
So what's it like just fronting Queen,

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:28.330
you've been doing it for
how long? Over a decade?

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:30.930
- It's been eight or nine years now.

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:33.630
Yeah, we met on the
finale of Idol actually.

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:34.650
- Oh my God, is that true?

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:36.430
I didn't know that that's
how that happened that has-

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:38.440
- Yeah, they came and performed

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:40.910
and it was like something
felt like it clicked,

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:41.890
but I couldn't tell you what

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:43.250
'cause you know how the finale is,

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:46.541
it's very, very confusing
and very chaotic.

00:02:46.541 --> 00:02:47.890
- Yeah.

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:49.270
- But then not too long afterwards,

00:02:49.270 --> 00:02:53.183
they got in touch with my team
and that's where it started.

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:56.497
Every time I get on
stage with them I'm like,

00:02:56.497 --> 00:02:59.340
"Oh my God, this job is the
coolest job in the world."

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:02.420
And, all the audiences
we see around the world

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:05.700
are so fantastic, they know
the words to every song,

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:08.160
they're dancing, there's
so much nostalgia floating

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:09.030
through the audience.
- Yeah.

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:12.580
- So, I'm very excited for
things to get back to normal

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:14.540
for us to be able to go on tour again.

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:17.650
- I know, I keep making that
joke that not one artist knew

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:20.560
that they were on their farewell tour,

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:22.646
the last tour they did (laughs) .

00:03:22.646 --> 00:03:25.780
'Cause we're not able to actually tour,

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:27.749
we didn't know it, we didn't.

00:03:27.749 --> 00:03:29.680
I mean, I gotta say this too,
you're not only makeup man,

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:32.460
you always look so fly,
so is it hard for you?

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:34.090
'Cause now we're only on Zoom,

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:37.220
and everybody can only see
this much of you, is it hard?

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:38.053
Do you think 'cause you
wanna get dressed up?

00:03:38.053 --> 00:03:39.160
- Yeah, it's weird.

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:41.560
I mean, on one hand it's sort of a relief,

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:44.294
because the quarantine 15 is real.

00:03:44.294 --> 00:03:45.127
(Kelly laughs)

00:03:45.127 --> 00:03:46.207
And-
- Yeah (laughs) .

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:49.500
- Chest up, I'm good with that (laughs).

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:50.840
- I don't know what you're referring to.

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:52.560
- Hiding the problem areas,

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:54.578
let's be honest, let's de-stigmatize it.

00:03:54.578 --> 00:03:55.433
(Kelly chuckles)

00:03:55.433 --> 00:04:00.433
But, I'm wearing sweat pants
with elastic waistbands

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:02.810
more than I ever have in my entire life.

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:05.217
- Oh, see, now when people started saying,

00:04:05.217 --> 00:04:06.860
"Oh I'm looking so relaxed all the time,"

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:09.190
I was like, "So welcome to my life,

00:04:09.190 --> 00:04:10.430
that it's been for like ever."

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:11.263
(Adam laughs)

00:04:11.263 --> 00:04:13.420
I was like, "There's no
new clothes are worn,

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:15.187
it's the same old clothes
that I'm wearing (laughs) ,"

00:04:15.187 --> 00:04:16.750
and it's like, "Well."

00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:19.300
But you were an Idol 12 years ago,

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:21.270
and I know we were on different seasons,

00:04:21.270 --> 00:04:23.140
and I know that they were
different things too,

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:26.520
like different times,
but I really enjoyed it,

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:30.020
and I enjoyed the comradery
within the contestants,

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:32.220
all the artists on
there. Did you enjoy it?

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:33.711
- I did, I had fun.

00:04:33.711 --> 00:04:34.544
- Me too.
- I had a great time.

00:04:34.544 --> 00:04:36.410
I mean, I was in a place in my life

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:38.250
where I had just sort of like...

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:41.260
I had this sort of shift
in the way I saw the world,

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:44.220
in that I realized very graphically

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:47.200
that I had been scared of so much.

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:49.880
Fear was holding me back in
a lot of areas in my life,

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:51.140
including my professional life.

00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:53.370
And, that year before I auditioned

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:56.927
I just kinda had this
breakthrough where I was like,

00:04:56.927 --> 00:04:58.820
"I'm not scared anymore.

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:00.140
I don't have anything to lose,

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:02.120
so I might as well
throw myself at things."

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:04.760
And, Idol was one of
the things that came up,

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:06.287
I saw the audition and I was like,

00:05:06.287 --> 00:05:08.910
"Yo why not? Keep saying
yes to as much as you can

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:10.480
and something will happen."

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:12.260
- Yeah, I feel like, I
don't know if you agree,

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:14.720
but I was, I know nobody
believes me when I say this

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:17.010
but I was actually pretty
shy when I was little,

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:19.990
and then once I started
getting on stage and singing,

00:05:19.990 --> 00:05:22.390
and kind of finding that, I
felt I was good at something,

00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:23.390
and it empowered me

00:05:23.390 --> 00:05:25.730
and gave me such a different
level of confidence.

00:05:25.730 --> 00:05:26.840
- So that's what. Do you feel like that?

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:29.050
Do you feel in life art
has kind of helped you

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:31.000
become who you are and
who you are meant to be?"

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:32.760
- Absolutely. Even if it's many things,

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:35.000
once you kind of find what you love,

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:36.623
it gives you a sense of self.

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:42.653
And I love shoes, I
love shoes for example.

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:46.460
Shoes really keep me on track.

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:47.750
- Wait, I love shoes too.

00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:50.260
Wait, this is a real thing.

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:52.520
I love shoes, but I'm talking Nikeys,

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:55.113
I have so many Nikeys in my closet.

00:05:56.224 --> 00:05:58.440
What kind of shoes are you referring to?

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:00.770
- Well, I like really the most extreme

00:06:00.770 --> 00:06:03.290
as I can possibly find, but also-

00:06:03.290 --> 00:06:04.123
- We're so opposite (laughs) .

00:06:04.123 --> 00:06:06.920
- I'm gonna be turning
40 in a year and a half,

00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:09.470
and sneakers are becoming a necessity.

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:11.907
But, I found these the other
day online and ordered them

00:06:11.907 --> 00:06:13.745
and I wanted to show them to you.

00:06:13.745 --> 00:06:15.910
- (sighs) Oh my God, no, but see,

00:06:15.910 --> 00:06:17.800
this is what's fabulous
about what you're holding.

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:20.510
Okay, see I'm down with what
you're holding and this is why.

00:06:20.510 --> 00:06:24.020
See, it looks like a huge
heel, but it's the platform,

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:27.180
your foot's not actually
doing this the entire time

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:28.130
Jimmy Choo.

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:30.260
- I mean they hurt (laughs) .

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:32.084
- They do? Now that I won't wear.

00:06:32.084 --> 00:06:32.917
- Yeah, they hurt.

00:06:32.917 --> 00:06:35.500
- Nope, I'm getting too
old for this thing now.

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:37.388
I look at myself and I'm just like, "No. "

00:06:37.388 --> 00:06:38.350
(both laugh)

00:06:38.350 --> 00:06:40.780
But they look fabulous (laughs) .

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:42.050
- I think they're like a moment

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:46.450
for one performance for
something where it's one song.

00:06:46.450 --> 00:06:47.283
- Yeah.

00:06:47.283 --> 00:06:49.076
- Maybe seated on a stool (laughs) .

00:06:49.076 --> 00:06:50.223
- (laughing) Exactly, exactly.

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:52.500
- Maybe (laughs) .

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:53.670
- I love it, I love it.

00:06:53.670 --> 00:06:55.290
Well, how do you feel?

00:06:55.290 --> 00:06:57.560
'Cause I feel like
you're a bit of a pioneer

00:06:57.560 --> 00:07:00.970
in the sense of being on Idol
especially, and being gay,

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:02.790
that was kind of a big thing.

00:07:02.790 --> 00:07:06.790
Being on the show and having
Middle America jump on board

00:07:06.790 --> 00:07:09.500
and vote, and I know
that had have been hard,

00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:11.160
I have friends in the same position.

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:13.460
So, how do you think things have changed

00:07:13.460 --> 00:07:15.930
for gay artists since 2009?

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:17.770
- Man, it's a big change.

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:20.640
I mean, I remember coming off of Idol,

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:23.960
and going into the mainstream
music industry label system,

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:24.860
and them just...

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:26.860
Even if they were excited themselves,

00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:28.920
there was a lot of question marks,

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:31.090
there was a lot of fear,
is this gonna sell?

00:07:31.090 --> 00:07:32.090
Is this gonna work?

00:07:32.090 --> 00:07:34.590
And as you know that's
the name of the game

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:37.720
for the commercial music
business, it's a business.

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:38.553
- Yeah.

00:07:38.553 --> 00:07:42.130
- So, it was a bit of a struggle
to kind of hold my ground

00:07:42.130 --> 00:07:45.270
and keep focused on my part of the deal

00:07:45.270 --> 00:07:47.713
which is being the
artist, being a musician,

00:07:48.890 --> 00:07:51.540
being the guy that
wears those weird shoes.

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:52.454
- Yeah.

00:07:52.454 --> 00:07:54.330
- It was a lot of ups and downs,

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:56.910
but I'm really thankful for my experience.

00:07:56.910 --> 00:07:59.890
And I think that the world has changed,

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:02.710
I think we're in a place now
within the music industry,

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:03.850
within the entertainment industry,

00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:07.830
and in the world where yes,
there's still discrimination,

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:10.120
there's still problems,
there's still work to be done,

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:13.710
but there has been so much progress made.

00:08:13.710 --> 00:08:15.880
- I agree, I think it's interesting,

00:08:15.880 --> 00:08:16.980
and one of the main reasons

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:18.340
why I signed up to do "The Voice"

00:08:18.340 --> 00:08:19.870
was because I'm turned around,

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:21.890
and I can't see what they look like,

00:08:21.890 --> 00:08:24.180
I don't know anything about them,

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:25.850
there are no preconceived notions.

00:08:25.850 --> 00:08:27.460
And I love like as a kid I was so poor,

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:28.370
I didn't have MTV,

00:08:28.370 --> 00:08:30.180
I didn't have any like
literally (speaks faintly)

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:32.050
my soul artists were maybe on award shows,

00:08:32.050 --> 00:08:33.930
if the one television
in our house was allowed

00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:35.060
to be on the awards that night.

00:08:35.060 --> 00:08:40.060
And so, I never saw anyone,
so I fell in love with music,

00:08:40.180 --> 00:08:41.190
how they sounded.

00:08:41.190 --> 00:08:42.240
It had nothing to do...

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:43.890
I had no idea if anybody was gay,

00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:45.350
I had no idea if they were whatever,

00:08:45.350 --> 00:08:47.180
I just fell in love with the music,

00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:49.630
and I think that that is so important,

00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:53.010
because that's the art, it's
the music, and it moves us.

00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:54.910
And, I don't care what package it comes in

00:08:54.910 --> 00:08:57.710
as long as I'm lucky
enough to get to hear it

00:08:57.710 --> 00:08:59.900
from whatever vessel
it comes out of but...

00:08:59.900 --> 00:09:02.290
Is it true that you're
writing your own musical?

00:09:02.290 --> 00:09:03.770
'Cause I find that fascinating.

00:09:03.770 --> 00:09:05.720
- Yeah, I am, I've been
working on it this year.

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:07.600
I mean, after...

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:09.900
I put out an album out
in March called "Velvet,"

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:12.740
and literally a week and a half later,

00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:13.930
everything got shut down,

00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:18.350
so I was like, "Oh, okay,
let's mourn the loss of that."

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:19.880
Obviously there's more
important things (mumbles)

00:09:19.880 --> 00:09:21.100
on the world than a pop album.

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:22.200
- Yeah.

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:26.930
- And then I kinda had gotten
into the works last year,

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:29.357
but when I had all this
downtime I was like,

00:09:29.357 --> 00:09:32.030
"Okay, now's the time
to really buckle down

00:09:32.030 --> 00:09:33.160
and work on this thing."

00:09:33.160 --> 00:09:35.600
And, I'm so excited about it (laughs) .

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:38.250
- But you're doing two live virtual shows

00:09:38.250 --> 00:09:39.740
on your birthday this Friday.

00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:42.821
So happy birthday, first
of all, that's exciting.

00:09:42.821 --> 00:09:43.654
- Thank you.

00:09:43.654 --> 00:09:46.020
- And also why two? I'm just curious.

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:47.690
Why are you doing two live shows?

00:09:47.690 --> 00:09:50.340
- Doing two because one's
at noon and one's at night,

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:52.545
that way people overseas can watch it.

00:09:52.545 --> 00:09:57.545
I'm very lucky to have
fans in Europe and UK,

00:09:57.850 --> 00:10:00.440
so I'm making it a global approach.

00:10:00.440 --> 00:10:01.437
- I was wondering, I was like,

00:10:01.437 --> 00:10:02.770
"Oh my God, you're
really Broadwaying it up

00:10:02.770 --> 00:10:04.700
with the matinee and the night,"

00:10:04.700 --> 00:10:05.903
and I was like, "that's amazing."

00:10:05.903 --> 00:10:06.736
(Adam laughs)

00:10:06.736 --> 00:10:07.620
I was like-
- A two show deck.

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:11.093
I know, and I haven't been
singing much lately, so I'm like,

00:10:11.093 --> 00:10:13.370
" (sighs) this is gonna
be a long day (laughs) ."

00:10:13.370 --> 00:10:16.660
- Oh my gosh. Well, good luck
and God's speed on that note.

00:10:16.660 --> 00:10:20.960
Be sure to check out Adam's
livestream show on January 29th

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:22.953
from the Roxy Theater in Los Angeles.

